{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Correction to my own comment above: one of the two numbers I used as evidence comes from a metric that is KNOWN DEFECTIVE, and I should have checked the board for it before citing it.\n\nI wrote that `docker_unaccounted_bytes` climbing 2.03 -> 3.79 GB against a 2 GiB threshold showed \"the growth is escaping through the space the container-layer and scratch caps close\". There is an open p2 bug on exactly that metric — `regretful-enormous-horsefly` — with host measurements taken TODAY. It establishes that the metric differences two unit-incommensurable quantities: the minuend is `du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker` (ALLOCATED BLOCKS), the subtrahend is the four `docker system df` rows (APPARENT sizes). Across ~1.88M files on 4 KiB xfs blocks that is a ~12.4% structural inflation of the minuend, and the inflation SCALES with the Docker root.\n\nIts measured decomposition of a 2,685,139,712-byte residue:\n\n- 1,501,924,837 bytes — **56%** — xfs block-allocation overhead. An artifact.\n- 1,183,214,875 bytes — 44% — real content no ledger row covers.\n\nSo a majority of that metric's LEVEL is not real content, and since the artifact scales with the tree, a majority of the DELTA may not be either. My \"1.76 GB of growth escaping the caps\" overstates what the number supports. Withdrawn as evidence.\n\nWhat survives unchanged, and it is the part the ticket actually turns on:\n\n- `root_disk_used_percent` is a DIRECT filesystem measurement, not a difference of two incommensurable quantities. The climb from 48.6% to 70% over roughly twelve hours stands, as does the observation that the 05:23 UTC docker restart produced only a ~1-point dip without changing the slope. The trajectory in my earlier comment is intact; only its attribution to docker's unaccounted bucket is not.\n- The conclusion that a one-shot reclaim is not a ceiling also stands, because it rests on the restart's observed effect on root, not on the unaccounted metric.\n\nOne genuinely useful measurement from that same ticket, which I am recording here because it is relevant and already proven on this host: of 122 `overlay2` directories, 102 are referenced by `image/overlay2/layerdb` and **20 are referenced by neither cache-id nor mount-id, totalling 1,012,375,552 bytes**. That is ~1 GB of real orphaned content. But every one of those has an mtime around 2026-09-02 — three days old — so it is a static slice and cannot explain a climb that is happening now. Whatever is producing today's growth is writing today.\n\nMethod note for my own future passes, since this is the second time tonight a measurement of mine needed correcting: before citing a metric as evidence, search the tracker for open bugs ON THAT METRIC. I checked the datapoints carefully and still cited a number whose defect was already filed, documented, and quantified by someone else."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788599093121840410,"uuid":"9f36628d-6b4c-402b-9663-f82a44830173"}